  about: [
    `<em>Treasure Island</em> is the story of Jim Hawkins, an innkeeper's son who finds a treasure map in a dead sailor's chest. The map leads to the Caribbean island where Captain Flint buried his fortune — and to Long John Silver, the ship's cook who recruited Flint's old crew for the voyage without the honest men aboard knowing it. Jim sails with Squire Trelawney and Dr. Livesey, discovers the mutiny before it begins, and spends the next two weeks surviving on the island through a series of decisions that, for a boy of fourteen, require a level of nerve the adults around him do not always manage. The book ends with the treasure found and Silver escaped — the only character whose fate the narrator cannot account for, and cannot forget.`,
    `Stevenson wrote the book in the summer of 1881 at his stepson Lloyd's request, working from a hand-drawn map of an imaginary island. It was serialized in <em>Young Folks</em> magazine in 1881–82 and published as a book in 1883. It is the founding adventure novel — the book that established what the form would look like for the next century. Every adventure story since — the desert island, the buried treasure, the double-crossing pirate, the boy who outperforms the adults — is in conversation with it. Stevenson himself knew what he was making: "no woman in the story, no love interest, no need of psychological machinery." The interest is action and character, and the character who dominates both is Silver.`,
    `Jim narrates in retrospect, from adulthood, writing at the request of the men who were there. The retrospective frame gives him full knowledge from the beginning — the early chapters plant details whose significance only becomes clear later, controlled entirely by Jim's narrative intelligence. There is one structural interruption: Chapters 16 through 18 are narrated by Dr. Livesey, giving the reader the events at the stockade during Jim's solo adventures elsewhere. When Jim's voice returns, the contrast is precise — a professional adult's tactical account against an adolescent's felt experience. The two registers are Stevenson's argument about how differently the same events look from inside and outside extremity.`,
  ],

  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 34 chapters — from the old sea-dog at the Admiral Benbow to the gold loaded aboard the Hispaniola.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>Treasure Island divides into six parts of unequal length. Part One (Ch. 1–6) is the Admiral Benbow and the treasure map. Part Two (Ch. 7–12) is Bristol, the ship, and the discovery of the mutiny. Part Three (Ch. 13–20) is the first days on the island — the stockade, the first battle, Ben Gunn. Part Four (Ch. 21–24) is the stockade attack and Jim's sea adventure. Part Five (Ch. 25–30) is the island's second half — Jim aboard the Hispaniola alone, then Silver's camp. Part Six (Ch. 31–34) is the treasure hunt and the ending. The pace accelerates in the final third: Stevenson designed it to move.</p>`,

  themesByline: 'Four threads through the novel',
